' bug-285, plan-136-C form: a function-level TRAP body sees the function body's
' locals, so a body local that shares a file PRIVATE's name must win inside the
' TRAP body too. The scope rewriter once seeded the trap block with params + the
' trap binding only, so `helper()` below was renamed to the file private and the
' trap printed 42 instead of 7 — adding an unrelated PRIVATE silently changed an
' existing function's behavior.
'
' plan-136-C forbids a local named like a top-level LET/MUT, so bug-285's
' original `LET x` vs `PRIVATE LET x` program is now a compile error
' (tests/syntax/trap/trap-body-local-shadows-private-invalid). Function names are
' not covered by that rule, so the same trap-body scoping is pinned here through
' a PRIVATE FUNC and a local lambda of the same name.
IMPORT io

PRIVATE FUNC helper() AS Integer
  RETURN 42
END FUNC

FUNC main() AS Integer
  LET helper = LAMBDA() -> 7
  LET bad AS Integer = toInt("nope")
  io::print(toString(bad))
  RETURN 1
  TRAP(e)
    io::print(toString(helper()))
    RETURN 0
  END TRAP
END FUNC
